WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
An open source ai just made a full video of an otter using a laptop on a plane, in three minutes, on a regular computer. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 136. Think about the last time you wanted to create something visual for a school project or a quick video for social media. A few years back, that meant sending your idea off to some distant company server and waiting for results. Now, an open weights model called MiniMax H3 can build the whole scene on you.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:58.880
In your own laptop, complete with sound in roughly three minutes. Open weights means the models numbers and rules sit out in the open for anyone to download and run locally. It works like getting the full recipe and every ingredient instead of ordering a finished dish from a restaurant kitchen. Ethan Mollick tested this by typing in a prompt about an otter using a laptop on an airplane. The output showed natural motion and matching audio generated together on ordinary consumer hardware.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:15.100
Less than two years ago, the same quality level would have required expensive remote data centers and per second fees. Running it at home keeps your prompts and finished clips completely private. No one else sees what you are experimenting with, which removes both cost and waiting.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:28.540
Picture a personal notebook where every sketch stays between the pages instead of being scanned by a shared scanner. That shift turns video creation into something students and hobbyists can explore without limits on an everyday machine.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:39.920
The real headline is how fast the progress happened. What once needed cloud time now runs at home, placing video tools in the same everyday category as text and image generators.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:53.680
If you have a reasonably powerful computer, you can download Mini Max H3 and test a simple scene yourself. Try typing something like a cat riding a skateboard in a park and watch how motion and sound come together.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:02:05.300
The experiment itself shows these tools moving from research demos to something you can run on your own device. Now let us look under the hood at how a full language model can fit inside a spreadsheet.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:18.780
You know how a recipe card lists every step and every measurement so nothing stays hidden? A spreadsheet version of a tiny GPT does exactly that, except each cell holds one tiny calculation in the full sequence.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:27.760
The creator built a working nano GPT with about 85,000 parameters and placed every multiplication and addition into visible cells.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:35.760
You can watch text turn into numbers. See those numbers get multiplied by weights and watch the final numbers become predicted words.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:42.720
Because everything lives in plain sight, you can click any cell and trace exactly which earlier cells fed into it.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:49.420
It feels like opening the hood of a car and finding every wire, bolt and connection labeled and reachable by hand.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:58.200
The spreadsheet recreates the full GPT architecture so every attention layer and feed forward step appears as ordinary spreadsheet formulas.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:05.580
Change one input number and watch how that single change ripples through dozens of later cells until it affects the final prediction.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:15.880
The point is not to use this tiny model for real work. The point is to remove the mystery by showing the same operations that happen at huge scale inside bigger systems.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:26.720
When you later see chat GPT or similar tools, you already recognize the basic pattern of turning words into numbers, doing lots of math and turning numbers back into words.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.900
That single spreadsheet makes the black box feel far less black.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:39.780
Once the small version makes sense, the large version stops seeming like magic and start looking like very large, very fast copies of the same recipe.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:45.300
Here is one free tool you can try right now that turns any city into a clean poster.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:52.700
An open source project lets you pick any place in the world and instantly generate a minimalist map poster from its street layout.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.500
No design skills are required because the tool pulls public map data and builds the image for you.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.540
Go to the tool website, type your hometown or a city you want to visit and hit generate.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:09.800
You can tweak line thickness or colors if you like, then download the result for printing or a phone background.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:15.360
It becomes a fun way to notice how streets form patterns you never spot when walking around them.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.800
Another free resource shows the whole family tree of neural networks in one clear graphic.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:27.260
Instead of just hearing the words neural network, you can see how different designs connect and branch out.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.620
Open the image and zoom into sections that interest you.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:36.720
You will notice some designs focus on images, others on text, and some try to combine both.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:43.760
It works like studying a subway map of AI ideas rather than trying to memorize every stop by name.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:48.420
One experiment compares three ways to give an AI its own computer.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:54.880
You can let it use your actual laptop, give it a temporary online machine that resets after each use,

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.240
or give it a persistent online machine that keeps files between sessions.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:03.320
Each choice changes what the AI can remember and do safely.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:08.780
The order on a plane clip took roughly three minutes to generate locally, with sound included.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:15.300
That speed on consumer hardware shows how quickly open video models are catching up to cloud only tools.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.180
And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:25.320
Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow.

